verification.corrected_claim:
Forge 2.0.13 applies a filter in AiController.getSpellAbilityToPlay, its voluntary priority chooser (used whenever simulation is off, which it always has been). The filter drops every spell and ability whose host card's script says AI:RemoveDeck:All.

Consequence: across all studies, the Forge AI (stock seats, and plan seats outside the shim's spell substitutions) never once voluntarily activated a non-mana ability of a flagged permanent. Examples: printed Clock of Omens 0 activations in 135 battlefield entries, against 344 activations of unflagged copies (339 Sculpting Steel); Quirion Ranger 0 in 56 entries; Aphetto Alchemist 0 in 45. Stock seats never cast a flagged spell by choice either: Demonic Consultation 0 casts in 69+ seat-games holding it.

The filter is bypassed by:
- the counterspell pre-pass, which runs before the filter (stock cast Swan Song 148 times and Pact of Negation 132 times);
- effect-driven free casts (cascade, the Expertise cycle, miracle);
- mana abilities used to pay costs;
- the shim, which casts flagged spells itself (Consultation 51 casts by plan seats) but never activates abilities.

In the 32 human-ceiling cEDH decks, 91 of 235 lines (46 of 122 globally unique, 25 of 32 decks) contain a flagged piece. Only 52 of those are hard-blocked for both seat types, because the flagged piece needs a non-mana activation. 10 are blocked only for stock seats, because the flagged piece is cast-only. In 29 the flagged piece has only mana abilities, and there the filter is not the operative block.

The flag is carried by a large minority of cEDH engines, not most. Flagged: Clock, LED, Consultation, Isochron Scepter, Food Chain, Dualcaster Mage, Doomsday, Ad Nauseam, Flash, KCI. Unflagged: Thassa's Oracle, Tainted Pact, Underworld Breach, Kiki-Jiki, Heliod, Walking Ballista, Dramatic Reversal, Portal to Phyrexia.
